---
title: "The allegory — FrontierJS"
description: "Running software is running a restaurant: the dish, the doors, the walk-in, the pass, the alarms, the prep crew, the menu, the keys, the suppliers, the bad night, the books and the second location, all cooked from one recipe card."
render: static
---

<!--
  The tools named here are the ones the-story names; a new one is checked
  there first. A chapter wearing a .stamp says its question was answered on
  the recipe card in 01, so a stamp on a chapter whose answer lives elsewhere
  is a false claim.
-->

<section id="top" class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The allegory</p>
    <h1>Building software is like opening a restaurant.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      On the first night you have a dish worth eating and somewhere to cook
      it. Every night after that brings something new to look after, and each
      one turns up because you dealt with the one before.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="rail-wrap" aria-labelledby="rail-title">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 id="rail-title" class="sr-only">The order rail</h2>
    <nav class="rail" aria-label="Chapters">
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-01"><span class="num">01 · Cook</span><strong>The dish</strong><span class="q">Does it work?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-02"><span class="num">02 · Deploy</span><strong>The doors</strong><span class="q">Can anyone get in?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-03"><span class="num">03 · Persist</span><strong>The walk-in</strong><span class="q">What if the fridge dies?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-04"><span class="num">04 · Observe</span><strong>The pass</strong><span class="q">Is tonight going well?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-05"><span class="num">05 · Alert</span><strong>The alarm</strong><span class="q">Will I hear when it isn't?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-06"><span class="num">06 · Automate</span><strong>Morning prep</strong><span class="q">Why am I doing this by hand?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-07"><span class="num">07 · Release</span><strong>The new menu</strong><span class="q">Can I change it safely?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-08"><span class="num">08 · Secure</span><strong>The keys</strong><span class="q">Who can touch what?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-09"><span class="num">09 · Integrate</span><strong>Suppliers</strong><span class="q">What if the fish doesn't come?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-10"><span class="num">10 · Recover</span><strong>The bad night</strong><span class="q">What happens at eight on a Saturday?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket" href="#ch-11"><span class="num">11 · Operate</span><strong>The books</strong><span class="q">Is the whole place healthy?</span></a>
      <a class="ticket last" href="#ch-12"><span class="num">12 · Again</span><strong>Second location</strong><span class="q">Do I do all of that twice?</span></a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapters">
  <div class="container">
    <ol class="arc">

      <li id="ch-01">
        <span class="n">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Does it work?</p>
          <h2>The dish</h2>
          <p>Every restaurant starts with one plate somebody wants to eat. Behind it is a recipe card: what goes in, how it's made, how it's served, and the line at the bottom that says who mustn't eat it.</p>
          <figure class="card" aria-label="A recipe card">
            <p class="card-title">Lead</p>
            <ul>
              <li><span>name</span><span>Text</span></li>
              <li><span>email</span><span>Email</span></li>
              <li><span>stage</span><span>new · won · lost</span></li>
              <li><span>owner</span><span>User</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p class="card-warn">@@gate: staff read, owner writes</p>
          </figure>
          <p>In FrontierJS the recipe card is <code>db/schema.lite</code>. You write a model once and the rest is cooked from it: the data (Model), the API that serves it (Service) and the screens people use (Resource). The line at the bottom goes on the card too. A <code>@@gate</code> on the model says who may read a record and who may change it.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Everything after this is cooked from the card.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/flow/">Flow</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-02">
        <span class="n">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Can anyone get in?</p>
          <h2>Open the doors</h2>
          <p>A dish nobody can reach isn't a restaurant. It needs an address, a sign, the lights on and a door that unlocks at six. Behind the door are the gas, the water and the power, and the meter room stays locked.</p>
          <p><code>fli deploy</code> puts the app on a server, and Outpost on that server acts only on signed instructions. Config is read in one place, through <code>$.config</code>, and <code>fli env:*</code> moves each environment's values between machines. Ports follow a formula of environment, category, project and service, so dev, test and prod never share a door.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Where it lives is part of the plan.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-03">
        <span class="n">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">What if the fridge dies?</p>
          <h2>Stock the walk-in</h2>
          <p>Everything the kitchen knows sits in the walk-in: the stock, the prep, the sauce that took two days. The night the compressor fails is the night you find out whether anything was kept anywhere else.</p>
          <p><code>fli db:backup</code> takes a hot copy of every database the schema declares, while it's serving. Migrations come from diffing the schema against the database, so the walk-in always matches the card. Restoring a copy is one command.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Keep a second copy of what you can't cook again.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-04">
        <span class="n">04</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Is tonight going well?</p>
          <h2>Watch the pass</h2>
          <p>A good manager stands where the plates come out. Which tickets are waiting, which tables have turned, which plate came back untouched. You can't fix a service you can't see.</p>
          <p>Every request carries a trace id from the door to the database and back. Health checks and metrics are registered next to the code they describe, and the devtools panel shows calls as they happen.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">See the service while it's still going on.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-05">
        <span class="n">05</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Will I hear when it isn't?</p>
          <h2>The alarm</h2>
          <p>Nobody can stand at the pass all night. So there's a smoke alarm, a fridge that beeps when it warms, and a cook who shouts when a ticket has waited forty minutes. The building tells you when something's wrong.</p>
          <p>Basecamp, itself a FrontierJS app, watches every server and every app on it and raises an alert when one stops answering or a check fails. Alerts are notifications, so each one lands in the app and in an inbox at once.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Hear it from the building, not from a customer.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-06">
        <span class="n">06</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Why am I doing this by hand?</p>
          <h2>Morning prep</h2>
          <p>Half the work of a kitchen happens when nobody's eating. The prep crew comes in at seven, the standing order goes out on Mondays, the floors get done after close. Nobody asks for it each day. It just happens, by the same rules as the rest of the kitchen.</p>
          <p>Caravan runs the queue and the schedule, on SQLite. A follow-up can be queued in the same transaction as the save that prompted it, so it only runs if the save did. A job reads and writes through the same gates as a person.</p>
          <p class="stamp"><a href="#ch-01">Settled at 01 · the card</a></p>
          <p class="takeaway">Work nobody clicked answers to the same card.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-07">
        <span class="n">07</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Can I change it safely?</p>
          <h2>Change the menu</h2>
          <p>Sooner or later the menu changes. You taste it on Tuesday, print it on Wednesday and keep last season's in the drawer, because a new dish that sinks on Friday has to come off by Saturday.</p>
          <p>A new menu comes from new cards. The migration is diffed from the schema, and <code>fli test:access</code> writes the whole access policy out as a snapshot, so a change to who can do what shows up in review. <code>fli proves</code> names the checks a change needs, <code>fli release:mint</code> shows exactly what would ship, and <code>fli deploy:rollback</code> puts the last release back.</p>
          <p class="stamp"><a href="#ch-01">Settled at 01 · the card</a></p>
          <p class="takeaway">Know what changes before anyone eats it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-08">
        <span class="n">08</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Who can touch what?</p>
          <h2>The keys</h2>
          <p>A busy restaurant ends up with a ring of keys: the walk-in, the till, the office safe, the wine store. People join and leave, and the question is never who has a key. It's whether anyone remembers who has a key.</p>
          <p>Here it was answered on the card. The <code>@@gate</code> on each model holds whether the caller is a person at the door, a job at dawn or an agent working through MCP. <code>@encrypted</code> and <code>@guarded</code> fields stay locked at the data layer, secrets never sit in the repo, and the audit trail records each change with protected fields written as <code>[redacted]</code>.</p>
          <p class="stamp"><a href="#ch-01">Settled at 01 · the card</a></p>
          <p class="takeaway">One lock, whoever's at the door.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-09">
        <span class="n">09</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">What if the fish doesn't come?</p>
          <h2>The suppliers</h2>
          <p>No kitchen grows its own fish. There's the fish guy, the bakery, the card machine and the delivery apps, and every one of them is a way for Friday to go wrong without anyone in the building doing anything.</p>
          <p>Each outside service is declared once, with its own timeouts and retries, and every call goes through <code>app.conduit.send()</code>. When something changes inside, webhooks go out at least once and try again when nobody answers. Sign-in with another provider comes through the same door.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Every supplier is on the books.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/seams/">Seams</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-10">
        <span class="n">10</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">What happens at eight on a Saturday?</p>
          <h2>The bad night</h2>
          <p>The oven dies with a full room. The card machine goes down. A delivery never turns up. A good kitchen has been through it before and knows the next move without stopping to think.</p>
          <p>A deploy that went wrong is one <code>fli deploy:rollback</code>, and the deploy journal says what happened. A lost database comes back from the last backup. A failed job retries on its own, and a supplier that's down is retried by conduit without anyone writing a loop.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Have the next move ready before you need it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-11">
        <span class="n">11</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Is the whole place healthy?</p>
          <h2>The books</h2>
          <p>One day you notice you don't cook much anymore. You do the rota, the books, the repairs and the inspections, and you keep a notebook of why the walk-in is where it is so nobody moves it. You're the owner now.</p>
          <p><code>fli</code> is the office desk. Its commands are written in markdown, and your project's own sit beside the framework's. <code>fli check</code> turns your conventions into checks that fail, so nobody has to remember them. The reasons live in <code>DECISIONS.md</code>, what's broken in <code>ISSUES.md</code>, and <code>fli next</code> suggests what to work on.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Less running around for the person who answers for all of it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="card" class="reveal">
        <span class="n">··</span>
        <div>
          <h2>One card, every station</h2>
          <p>Write "contains nuts" on the card once, and nobody has to remember to say it again.</p>
          <div class="stations" aria-label="Where one line on the card turns up">
            <div class="station s-menu"><b>Menu</b><span>marks the dish</span></div>
            <div class="station s-ticket"><b>Ticket</b><span>flags the order</span></div>
            <div class="hub"><span>the card</span><b>contains nuts</b></div>
            <div class="station s-floor"><b>Floor</b><span>warns the table</span></div>
            <div class="station s-insp"><b>Inspector</b><span>checks the line</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>The <code>@@gate</code> on Lead works the same way. You wrote it once, and it turns up at every station:</p>
          <table class="views">
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Screens</th><td>the query refuses rows the caller can't see, and the screen hides the button</td></tr>
              <tr><th>API</th><td>a refused call gets its status from the one place that maps errors</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Prep crew</th><td>a job reads through the same gate as a person</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Agents</th><td>an agent isn't offered a tool it isn't allowed to use</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Review</th><td><code>fli test:access</code> puts any change to it in the diff</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="takeaway">One card for the whole kitchen.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="ch-12">
        <span class="n">12</span>
        <div>
          <p class="asks">Do I do all of that twice?</p>
          <h2>The second location</h2>
          <p>Then it goes well enough that you open another. Without the cards, you rebuild the kitchen from memory and every station comes out slightly different. With them, you open the binder.</p>
          <figure class="branches" aria-label="Two restaurants opened from one binder">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 80" class="shop" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M10 30 h100 v45 h-100 z" />
              <path d="M6 30 l8 -16 h92 l8 16" />
              <path d="M6 30 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0" />
              <path d="M48 75 v-26 h24 v26" />
              <path d="M18 44 h22 v16 h-22 z M80 44 h22 v16 h-22 z" />
            </svg>
            <div class="binder"><b>db/schema.lite</b><span>the binder</span></div>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 80" class="shop" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M10 30 h100 v45 h-100 z" />
              <path d="M6 30 l8 -16 h92 l8 16" />
              <path d="M6 30 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0 q9 8 18 0" />
              <path d="M48 75 v-26 h24 v26" />
              <path d="M18 44 h22 v16 h-22 z M80 44 h22 v16 h-22 z" />
            </svg>
          </figure>
          <p>Every FrontierJS app is laid out the same way: <code>db/</code> at the root and each surface beside it, <code>api/</code>, <code>web/</code>, <code>site/</code>. <code>fli new</code> opens the next one already set up, Outpost runs on each server, and Basecamp runs every location from one office.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">The second one opens as a restaurant, not a dish.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

    </ol>

    <section class="kitchens" aria-labelledby="kitchens-title">
      <h2 id="kitchens-title">Eleven jobs, two kitchens</h2>
      <div class="pair">
        <div class="kitchen loose">
          <p class="k-head">Every job brings its own</p>
          <ol>
            <li><b>Dish</b><span>a framework</span></li>
            <li><b>Doors</b><span>a host</span></li>
            <li><b>Walk-in</b><span>a backup script</span></li>
            <li><b>Pass</b><span>a log service</span></li>
            <li><b>Alarm</b><span>a pager</span></li>
            <li><b>Prep</b><span>a cron box</span></li>
            <li><b>Menu</b><span>a CI file</span></li>
            <li><b>Keys</b><span>an auth vendor</span></li>
            <li><b>Suppliers</b><span>six SDKs</span></li>
            <li><b>Bad night</b><span>a runbook</span></li>
            <li><b>Books</b><span>a spreadsheet</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="kitchen bound">
          <p class="k-head">Every job reads the card</p>
          <p class="spine-top">db/schema.lite</p>
          <ol>
            <li><b>Dish</b><span>Model · Service · Resource</span></li>
            <li><b>Doors</b><span>fli deploy</span></li>
            <li><b>Walk-in</b><span>fli db:backup</span></li>
            <li><b>Pass</b><span>traces · health</span></li>
            <li><b>Alarm</b><span>Basecamp</span></li>
            <li><b>Prep</b><span>Caravan</span></li>
            <li><b>Menu</b><span>fli release:mint</span></li>
            <li><b>Keys</b><span>@@gate</span></li>
            <li><b>Suppliers</b><span>conduit</span></li>
            <li><b>Bad night</b><span>fli deploy:rollback</span></li>
            <li><b>Books</b><span>fli</span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="closing">
      <p class="punch">You thought you were cooking.<br />You were running a restaurant.</p>
      <p>FrontierJS is a framework for building and running software systems, for the few people who look after the whole place.</p>
      <p class="see">The same arc, with the tools up front: <a href="/the-story/">The story</a> · <a href="/map/">Map</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero { padding: 4.5rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .eyebrow { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); letter-spacing: -.03em; margin: .3em 0; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.12rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 60ch; line-height: 1.6; }
  .sr-only { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; }

  /* The rail is a background stripe repeating at the row pitch, so each wrapped
     row of tickets hangs from its own bar at any width. Pitch = ticket height
     + row gap; changing one without the other floats the tickets off the bar. */
  .rail-wrap { padding: 1rem 0 2.5rem; }
  .rail {
    --ticket-h: 7.5rem; --row-gap: 1.5rem; --pitch: calc(var(--ticket-h) + var(--row-gap));
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(8.5rem, 1fr));
    column-gap: .8rem; row-gap: var(--row-gap);
    padding-top: .4rem;
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--rule-strong) 0 .25rem, transparent .25rem var(--pitch));
  }
  .ticket {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .2rem;
    height: var(--ticket-h); overflow: hidden;
    padding: .55rem .7rem .7rem;
    background: var(--surface-raised); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-top: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
    text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: .76rem; line-height: 1.35;
    transform-origin: top center; transform: rotate(-1.2deg);
    transition: transform .15s ease, border-color .15s ease;
  }
  .ticket:nth-child(even) { transform: rotate(.9deg); }
  .ticket:nth-child(3n) { transform: rotate(-.4deg); }
  .ticket:hover, .ticket:focus-visible { transform: none; border-color: var(--color-primary); }
  .ticket .num { font-size: .66rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); padding-bottom: .3rem; border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--rule); }
  .ticket strong { color: var(--ink); font-size: .9rem; font-weight: 600; font-family: var(--font-primary); }
  .ticket.last { border-color: var(--color-primary); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .ticket { transition: none; } }

  .chapters { padding: 1rem 0 5rem; }
  .arc { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .arc > li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; padding: 1.6rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--sticky-offset) + 1rem); }
  .arc .n { font-size: .78rem; letter-spacing: .14em; color: var(--ink-mute); padding-top: .3rem; }
  .arc h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  .arc p { margin: 0; max-width: 64ch; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .arc p + p, .arc table + p, .arc p + table, .arc figure + p, .arc p + figure, .arc div + p, .arc p + div { margin-top: .55rem; }
  .arc .asks { font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace); font-size: .74rem; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: .15rem; }
  .takeaway { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .see { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .reveal h2 { color: var(--color-primary); }

  /* A rubber stamp: the question was answered on the card back in 01. */
  .stamp a {
    display: inline-block; transform: rotate(-2deg);
    padding: .15rem .55rem; border: 2px solid var(--color-primary); border-radius: 4px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace); font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-primary); text-decoration: none;
  }
  .stamp a:hover, .stamp a:focus-visible { transform: none; }

  /* The recipe card: ruled lines are the background, so every row and the
     title are pinned to the 1.6rem pitch and the .8rem + 1.7rem header, or the
     text floats between the lines. */
  .card {
    max-width: 24rem; margin: .9rem 0 0; padding: .8rem 1rem .4rem;
    background:
      linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 2.5rem, var(--color-danger) 2.5rem calc(2.5rem + 1px), transparent calc(2.5rem + 1px)),
      repeating-linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 0 calc(1.6rem - 1px), var(--rule) calc(1.6rem - 1px) 1.6rem) 0 2.5rem / 100% calc(100% - 2.5rem) no-repeat,
      var(--surface-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 3px;
    box-shadow: 0 1px 0 var(--rule), 0 6px 14px -10px rgba(0, 0, 0, .3);
    transform: rotate(-.6deg);
    font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace); font-size: .8rem;
  }
  .card .card-title { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 700; font-size: .95rem; height: 1.7rem; margin: 0; }
  .card ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .card li { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; height: 1.6rem; align-items: flex-end; padding-bottom: .15rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .card li span:last-child { color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .card .card-warn { display: flex; align-items: flex-end; height: 1.6rem; padding-bottom: .15rem; margin: 0; color: var(--color-danger); font-weight: 600; }

  /* One line on the card, four stations around it. */
  .stations {
    display: grid; gap: .6rem; max-width: 34rem; margin-top: .9rem;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.2fr 1fr;
    grid-template-areas: "menu . ticket" ". hub ." "floor . insp";
    position: relative;
  }
  .stations::before {
    content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 1.4rem 18%;
    border: 1px dashed var(--rule-strong); border-radius: 6px; z-index: 0;
  }
  .station, .hub { position: relative; z-index: 1; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .1rem; padding: .55rem .7rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; background: var(--surface); font-size: .8rem; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .station b { color: var(--ink); font-size: .88rem; }
  .s-menu { grid-area: menu; } .s-ticket { grid-area: ticket; } .s-floor { grid-area: floor; } .s-insp { grid-area: insp; }
  .hub { grid-area: hub; align-items: center; text-align: center; background: var(--surface-raised); border-color: var(--color-danger); font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace); }
  .hub b { color: var(--color-danger); font-size: .9rem; }

  .views { border-collapse: collapse; max-width: 64ch; width: 100%; font-size: .92rem; }
  .views th { text-align: left; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); padding: .35rem 1rem .35rem 0; white-space: nowrap; vertical-align: top; }
  .views td { color: var(--ink-soft); padding: .35rem 0; }
  .views tr + tr > * { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }

  /* Two storefronts drawn in currentColor, so the theme colors them. */
  .branches { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto 1fr; align-items: center; gap: .8rem; max-width: 34rem; margin: .9rem 0 0; }
  .shop { width: 100%; height: auto; color: var(--ink-soft); fill: none; stroke: currentColor; stroke-width: 1.6; stroke-linejoin: round; }
  .binder { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: .1rem; padding: .6rem .8rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-primary); border-left-width: 6px; border-radius: 4px; background: var(--surface-raised); font-size: .75rem; color: var(--ink-mute); }
  .binder b { font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace); color: var(--ink); font-size: .8rem; }

  /* The two kitchens: the left list has nothing in common, the right hangs
     every row off one spine that starts at the card. */
  .kitchens { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 2rem 0 1rem; }
  .kitchens h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .pair { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1.5rem; }
  .kitchen ol { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .35rem; }
  .kitchen li { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: .8rem; padding: .4rem .65rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 4px; background: var(--surface); font-size: .82rem; }
  .kitchen li b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .kitchen li span { color: var(--ink-mute); font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace); font-size: .74rem; text-align: right; }
  .k-head { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
  .loose li:nth-child(odd) { margin-left: .5rem; transform: rotate(.6deg); }
  .loose li:nth-child(3n) { margin-left: 0; margin-right: .5rem; transform: rotate(-.8deg); border-style: dashed; }
  .bound .spine-top { display: inline-block; margin: 0 0 .5rem; padding: .3rem .6rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-primary); border-radius: 4px; font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace); font-size: .78rem; color: var(--ink); background: var(--surface-raised); }
  .bound ol { position: relative; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .bound ol::before { content: ''; position: absolute; left: .45rem; top: -.5rem; bottom: 1rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--color-primary); }
  .bound li { position: relative; border-color: var(--rule-strong); }
  .bound li::before { content: ''; position: absolute; left: -.75rem; top: 50%; width: .75rem; border-top: 2px solid var(--color-primary); }

  .closing { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 2rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
  .closing .punch { font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3.4vw, 1.9rem); letter-spacing: -.02em; line-height: 1.3; color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .closing p { max-width: 52ch; margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
  .closing .punch + p { font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-soft); }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .pair { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .stations { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; grid-template-areas: "hub hub" "menu ticket" "floor insp"; }
    .stations::before { display: none; }
    .branches { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
    .branches .binder { grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 2; }
  }
</style>
